# Historical basis: Ervand Abrahamian, Iran Between Two Revolutions, ch. 5,
# pp. 249–250; Mostafa Elm, Oil, Power, and Principle, ch. 3,
# “The Constitution.”
# The allocation of legal work and all effects are counterfactual abstractions.
title: Emergency Legal Work
new-page: true
is-card: true
tags: party_affairs
view-if: emergency_legal_work_timer <= 0 and front_formed = 0 and year = 1949 and month >= 2
on-arrival: month_actions += 1; emergency_legal_work_timer = 4

= Emergency Legal Work

Martial law, arrests, the suppression of the
{!<span class="term term-left">!}Tudeh Party{!</span>!}, and restrictions
on political activity have created more cases than the opposition's lawyers
can pursue together. Some require immediate representation; others require a
record that can outlast the emergency.

The lawyers can concentrate on one part of that burden this month.

- @legal_detentions
- @legal_press
- @legal_archive
- @return_card

@legal_detentions
title: Prepare detention cases
subtitle: Individual files give the emergency a human and legal record, even where immediate release remains beyond reach
on-arrival: {!
Q.emergency_legal_work_repeat =
    Q.emergency_legal_work_last === "detentions" ? 1 : 0;
Q.emergency_legal_work_last = "detentions";
Q.constitutional_legitimacy += Q.emergency_legal_work_repeat ? 1 :
    (Q.constitutional_legitimacy < 70 ? 3 : 1);
Q.parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy +=
    Q.emergency_legal_work_repeat ? 1 :
    (Q.parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy < 70 ? 2 : 1);
!}

Lawyers collect orders, dates, family testimony, and the authority claimed for
each detention. The files do not compel the government to reverse itself, but
they prevent arrests from becoming an unexamined administrative fact.

The opposition acquires cases it can defend in constitutional language rather
than relying on a general denunciation alone.
[? if emergency_legal_work_repeat = 1:
Counsel return to the detention files already opened, adding new orders,
answers, and family testimony. The continuing work matters to those cases,
but it no longer establishes an entirely new constitutional argument. ?]
[? if constitutional_legitimacy >= 75: The principal detention arguments are
now preserved. Further work keeps individual files current, but adds less to
the general constitutional case. ?]

- @post_event: Continue

@legal_press
title: Defend the restricted newspapers
subtitle: Editors receive a common legal argument while deciding independently how much danger they can accept
on-arrival: {!
Q.emergency_legal_work_repeat =
    Q.emergency_legal_work_last === "press" ? 1 : 0;
Q.emergency_legal_work_last = "press";
Q.press_capacity += Q.emergency_legal_work_repeat ? 1 :
    (Q.press_capacity < 70 ? 3 : 1);
Q.constitutional_legitimacy += Q.emergency_legal_work_repeat ? 1 :
    (Q.constitutional_legitimacy < 70 ? 2 : 1);
!}

Counsel prepares a short brief on closures, censorship, and the legal limits
claimed for martial law. Editors adapt it to their own circumstances rather
than waiting for a central instruction that does not exist.

Several papers are better able to explain the dispute. Their ability to print
still depends on decisions the lawyers do not control.
[? if emergency_legal_work_repeat = 1:
Counsel update the brief for newer restrictions and individual cases. Editors
gain a current answer, while the basic argument—and the authorities' power to
prevent publication—remains much as before. ?]
[? if press_capacity >= 75: The available papers already have the common
argument. Another brief helps them answer a new order, but cannot restore
papers the authorities have prevented from printing. ?]

- @post_event: Continue

@legal_archive
title: Keep a common emergency archive
subtitle: A quiet documentary record sacrifices immediate publicity for evidence that can be used later
on-arrival: {!
Q.emergency_legal_work_repeat =
    Q.emergency_legal_work_last === "archive" ? 1 : 0;
Q.emergency_legal_work_last = "archive";
Q.parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy +=
    Q.emergency_legal_work_repeat ? 1 :
    (Q.parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy < 70 ? 3 : 1);
Q.organizational_reach += Q.emergency_legal_work_repeat ? 1 :
    (Q.organizational_reach < 70 ? 2 : 1);
!}

A small group catalogs decrees, newspaper orders, detention reports, and
contradictory official statements. Items that cannot be verified are marked
as such instead of being discarded or promoted into fact.

The archive attracts little attention this month. It gives future
constitutional and parliamentary work a firmer record.
[? if emergency_legal_work_repeat = 1:
The archivists add the latest orders and testimony to the system already
established. Continuity protects the record from dispersal, although another
month of cataloguing does not by itself broaden the opposition or alter the
emergency. ?]
[? if parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy >= 75: The archive's method is now
established. Continued cataloguing preserves new material, but does not by
itself broaden the opposition or change the emergency. ?]

- @post_event: Continue
